diff --git a/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md b/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
index bdc77b1..6d4e0dd 100644
--- a/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
+++ b/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@ See `diagrams/read-path-sequence.mermaid`.
 
 1. **Resolve the home zone (L2).** Resolve the path/key against the global namespace → file identity, **home zone**, and an ACL check (cached after first use). Consistency-sensitive reads route to the home zone (section 6.6); stale-tolerant reads may target a nearer replica.
 2. **Fetch the chunk map (L4).** From the home zone's metadata store — or a version-fenced replica — get the chunk list, fragment locations, and EC scheme (cached after first read). In steady state both metadata hops are cached, so a hot read goes straight to the data path.
+   A chunk map is **one metadata value**, and a value has a ceiling (100 KB on the tightest backend in play), so a large object's map is **segmented**: the inode root carries a segment-group identity (`{nonce, epoch}`) plus a segment table, and each segment's chunks live in their own `seg:<nonce>:<epoch>:<index>` record. Resolving it is the root plus **one bounded range read** of that group's segments, ordered by the segment index the resolver parses out — never a whole-namespace scan. **Every** consumer of a chunk map resolves it through the same call: the read path, GC's reference build, scrub, reconstruction, rebalance, backfill and post-restore reconciliation, so no representation of an object's bytes can be understood by one process and opaque to another. A segment that is absent while the root still names its generation is a **fail-closed error**, never a partial map; a segment that is absent because the root has moved on (a concurrent supersede or delete) means the generation was retired, and the reader restarts against the current root or answers "no such key".
 3. **Read fragments directly from D servers.** Holding the EC logic, the client needs only *k* of *n* fragments and reconstructs from whichever *k* arrive first — turning erasure coding into a tail-latency *advantage*: a slow or dead disk does not slow the read.
 4. **Verify checksums** on every fragment, catching bit rot at read time; re-read elsewhere on failure.
 
diff --git a/docs/design/architecture/08-crosscutting-concepts.md b/docs/design/architecture/08-crosscutting-concepts.md
index 0babc7e..e30b484 100644
--- a/docs/design/architecture/08-crosscutting-concepts.md
+++ b/docs/design/architecture/08-crosscutting-concepts.md
@@ -80,6 +80,8 @@ A half-upgraded fleet is the normal state during a rolling upgrade (constraint 2
 
 **Two compatibility axes.** *Wire*: every inter-component contract is versioned protobuf (`proto`), evolved by addition — neighbours interoperate across at least a one-version gap, so fields are never repurposed and removals lag deprecation by a release. *On-disk*: the chunk/fragment format carries its own version and EC-scheme id (ADR-0002, ADR-0019); a reader accepts every format version it claims to support, because data outlives the software that wrote it — old-format data is read, never rejected.
 
+**Metadata records evolve the same way, and one record's shape is load-bearing for it.** An inode's chunk map is either **flat** (the chunk list inline, a JSON array — the shape every record written before segmentation carries) or **segmented** (a JSON object: the segment-group identity `{nonce, epoch}`, a segment count, and the segment table). The two are told apart by JSON *type*, so a pre-existing record decodes and re-encodes **byte-identically** — which is a correctness requirement, not hygiene: every metadata compare-and-swap in the system is `require(key, encode(prior))` compared byte-for-byte against the stored value, so an encoding that gained a tag or a wrapper would turn every overwrite, backfill, reconstruction and rebalance of every pre-existing object into a permanent conflict. Two record classes accompany the segmented shape: `seg:<nonce>:<epoch>:<index>` holds one segment's chunks and the byte span it covers, and `seggrp:<nonce>` is a presence-only reservation marker that makes a group nonce unrepeatable. Both are **new prefixes** over which nothing older reads, so their introduction needs no migration and rewrites no record. The segment records are keyed by an independent group nonce rather than by the publishing session's id — they outlive that session — and by the fence **epoch** of the attempt that wrote them, so a rolled-back attempt's segments occupy a key range disjoint from any later attempt's and can never be reclaimed out from under a published object. Structural invariants of the segmented shape (the segment count, a gap-free ascending index sequence, a contiguous byte tiling, a well-formed nonce) are enforced **at decode**: a malformed record is an error, never a value a consumer could half-resolve.
+
 **Tested, not hoped.** Skew is exercised under the deterministic-simulation harness (ADR-0009): a simulated zone runs mixed-version nodes from a seed and asserts the commit protocol and read path stay correct across the gap. On-disk compatibility is pinned by conformance vectors per format version (`specs/conformance/`) that every reader must accept, and a mixed-version matrix in CI gates the one-version-gap guarantee (Q8) — the structural complement to the load/fault scenarios in section 10.
 
